# SYNOPSIS

```usage
vx elf2efi in.elf out.efi
vx mkbootfs out.img name=file ...
vx check-pixels file.ppm x,y,r,g,b ...
vx qemu-monitor socket command ...
vx mathgen [out.swift]
```

# DESCRIPTION

These commands are steps of the build.
CMake and `tools/qemu.sh` run them, from any directory; each reads and writes only the paths it is given.

## elf2efi

`vx elf2efi` converts the loader, linked as an ELF static PIE, to a PE32+ EFI application, because LLVM has no RISC-V COFF backend.
The input must be a little-endian ELF64 of type `ET_DYN` for x86-64, AArch64 or RISC-V, linked at address 0, with page-aligned `PT_LOAD` segments at or above 0x1000, leaving room for the PE headers.
No segment may be both writable and executable.
Every dynamic relocation must be the arch's `R_*_RELATIVE`; each becomes a base relocation of type `IMAGE_REL_BASED_DIR64` that the firmware applies when it loads the image.
Anything else is refused with a message such as `unsupported dynamic relocation type 7`.

## mkbootfs

`vx mkbootfs` writes a boot image in the format of bootfs(6).
Each argument after <out.img> is `name=file`: the file's contents are stored under <name>, which is non-empty, doesn't start with `/` and is at most 255 bytes.
Names must be distinct.
Files with the same contents share one copy.

## check-pixels

`vx check-pixels` reads a binary PPM (`P6`, 8 bits a channel, as QEMU's `screendump` writes) and compares pixels.
Each <x,y,r,g,b> expects the pixel at column x, row y to be the colour r, g, b.
It prints a line for each pixel that is off the screen or different, then `<width>x<height>: ok` or `<width>x<height>: FAILED`.
It exits 0 only if every pixel matched.

## qemu-monitor

`vx qemu-monitor` connects to QEMU's human monitor on the Unix <socket>, sends the rest of its arguments as one command line, waits for the `(qemu) ` prompt, and prints the reply.
It waits at most 10 seconds for any one read, and 200 ms at the end for QEMU to finish writing any file the command made, as `screendump` does.

The command `backtrace [start end link]` is its own.
For every CPU it prints the program counter and the return addresses along the frame-pointer chain (rbp on amd64, x29 on arm64), at most 24 deep, read through the monitor with that CPU's page tables.
With the kernel's randomised placement, give <start> and <end> of the kernel image and its <link> address (the numbers of the kernel's `kaslr:` console line, in hex): addresses inside the image are then printed as link addresses, which `llvm-symbolizer` can read against `build/<arch>/kernel/kernel.elf`.
On arm64 the pointer-authentication bits of a return address are cleared first.

## mathgen

`vx mathgen` computes the constants the libm needs, and writes them as Swift to <out.swift>, `Constants.swift` by default.
The tree's copy is `system/libc/math/generated/Constants.swift`.
π comes from Machin's formula, ln 2 and ln 10 from atanh series, each as a fixed-point integer of 1600 fraction bits; 2/π by long division.
From them it writes correctly rounded doubles, the Cody and Waite splits that argument reduction needs (a high part with trailing zeros, so a product with it is exact, and the remainder), and the first 1216 fraction bits of 2/π, in hex, for Payne and Hanek reduction of large arguments.
It prints `vx mathgen: wrote <path>`.

# EXAMPLES

```rc
% vx mkbootfs boot.img bin/hello=hello
% vx qemu-monitor /tmp/mon.sock screendump /tmp/screen.ppm
% vx check-pixels /tmp/screen.ppm 0,0,0,0,0
```

